            <div xml:id="d303">

                <pb facs="0320" n="316" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg316"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1719: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-10-13T11:00:00+09:00">October 13, 1938—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 13—3:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>663. Department’s 348, October 8, noon,<anchor n="34" xml:id="d303-f1"/> Shanghai
                    Northern Settlement.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>With the consent of the British Ambassador we are sending <pb facs="0321"
                            n="317" xml:id="pg317"/> by safe hand to the American Consul General at
                        Shanghai the substance of our 633<anchor n="35" xml:id="d303-f2"/> as well
                        as the substance of the present.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Craigie states that he fully appreciates the difficulty in acquiescing in
                        judicial appointments to the courts of the Settlement by the puppet
                        government at Shanghai but he is still exploring the possibility of some
                        compromise. He is taking the position that the present judges in the courts
                        of the Settlement are honest impartial men without political bias but he is
                        at present considering the possibility of working out a return during the
                        period of the emergency to the consular courts with Chinese
                        assessors.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
